## CANCELLED AS INVALID, 2026-08-31 — the defect does not exist

Filed by me (coordinator) on lane-unused's report during B108. Both of us were
wrong, and the mechanism is worth more than the ticket was.

### How it was filed

The b025 fixture carries this comment:

    // Before the fix the LSP typed these expressions as the method's return
    // type and emitted
    //   error: argument 1 of `CoroutineFunc`: expected `void ()`, got `void`

**"Before the fix."** That is B025's own historical record of the bug B025
FIXED. The lane read it as evidence of a live defect and reported it with
enough confidence that I filed a ticket on its word without checking the code.
Neither of us measured; we both reasoned from prose, and the prose was
describing the past.

### What the path actually does today — MEASURED

Real diagnostics dumped from both funcdef fixtures:

    b025 Main.as:49  argument 1 of `CoroutineFunc`:         expected `void ()`,    got `bool ()`
    b025 Main.as:51  argument 1 of `CoroutineFuncUserdata`: expected `void (ref)`, got `void ()`
    b025 Main.as:53  argument 1 of `CoroutineFunc`:         expected `void ()`,    got `string`

Line 49 is a method reference returning bool and renders `bool ()` — the
SIGNATURE, not the flattened `bool`. Line 51 likewise. Line 53 is a property
read, whose type genuinely IS the fact about it. No flattening anywhere.

Structure confirms the measurement rather than substituting for it:
`check_funcdef_ctor_arg` guards with `clearly_not_fn || !sigs.is_empty()`, so
when the argument resolves to any function `render_func_or_type` renders the
signature, and the type display is reached only when the argument is provably
NOT a callable — where its type is the correct answer.

### The other three emission sites, checked because B108 taught us the title's
### scope is not the defect's scope

There are FOUR `ArgTypeMismatch` emission sites, not the one the ticket named.
The other three (3818, 3860, 4190) are guarded to primitive-to-primitive or
Named-to-Named pairs. A callable types as `Funcdef(_)` or as a return type;
`TypeRepr::Void` is its own variant and not a `Primitive`, and a bare function
name is `Funcdef`, not `Named` — so a callable cannot reach any of those three
arms at all. The family does not flatten callables anywhere.

My prediction when assigning this was "the same answer as B108, method
references only". The real answer is narrower still: NO shape loses information
on this path, because B025 already fixed the one that did. Expecting-is-not-
measuring cut against the ticket EXISTING, not merely against its scope.

### A real finding, deliberately NOT actioned

There is a genuine cross-diagnostic spelling inconsistency:

  * `ArgTypeMismatch` renders `void ()` WITH a space (`render_funcdef_sig`,
    `render_func_or_type`)
  * `NoMatchingSignatures` renders `void()` without (via
    `funcdef_binding_source_display`, settled in B108)

Each diagnostic is INTERNALLY consistent — on an `ArgTypeMismatch` line both
`expected` and `got` use the same spelling, so nothing looks wrong to a reader.

Leave it alone. This is user-facing wording in a repo that pins wording against
game output, the ground truth for this particular message is OURS rather than
the compiler's, and probing is blocked under B097. Changing it on our own
aesthetic judgement is how a repo acquires a second spelling of the same idea —
the exact hazard B108 avoided. If it is ever done it needs its own ticket with
the ground truth attached.

### Left behind

A characterisation test pinning that `got` renders `bool ()` for a
bool-returning method reference. It PASSES on master, which is normally the
signature of a worthless test — but here "this is already correct" is precisely
the claim, so the test converts a verbal claim into an executable one and stops
a silent regression. That is the legitimate exception to the batch's
passes-for-free rule: the assertion carries positive content and would fail if
the rendering ever flattened.